On Wed, Oct 24, 2012 at 8:20 PM, Jacob Errington <nictytan@gmail.com> wrote:

> Although I don't fully follow what it is you're trying to get at here,
> it's generally agreed upon that using ka that way is no good. In general
> use, ka is used only for "pure functions" -- properties and not qualitites,
> which is how the CLL describes ka.


Okay.


That being said, I'd just use {nu}, which I believe represents an
> particular instance of a predication, which is just a property with the
> ce'u-places filled in:
>
> {lo nu lo fagri cu glare cu pluka mi}
> or perhaps,
> {lo nu seli'i lo fagri cu glare cu pluka mi}
>

The second one is interesting.  Instead of "nu seli'i", why not just
"li'i"?  Is "nu" rquired by pluka?

